Question 1

Among the stars in the following table (equinox 2000), write which one is, or which ones are:

a) Those belonging to the southern celestial hemisphere;

b) Those that are never seen in Santiago ($\phi = 33^\circ$ S);

c) Those that are circumpolar visible in Montevideo ($\phi = 35^\circ$ S);

d) Those that make their meridian passage closest to the zenith in Mexico City ($\phi = 19^\circ$ N);

e) The one that makes its meridian crossing on 21 March at the time closest to 2:30 am (local time) in Rio de Janeiro ($\phi = 23^\circ$ S; $\lambda = 43^\circ$ W).

$$\begin{array}{|l|c|c|}\hline \text{Star} & \text{Right ascension } (AR) & \text{Declination } (\delta) \\ \hline \text{Peacock } (\alpha\text{-Pav}) & 20\text{h } 25\text{min } 39\text{s} & -56^\circ 44' 07.4'' \\ \hline \text{Alderamin } (\alpha\text{-Cep}) & 21\text{h } 18\text{min } 34.9\text{s} & +62^\circ 35' 06.4'' \\ \hline \text{Algeiba } (\gamma\text{-Leo}) & 10\text{h } 19\text{min } 58.7\text{s} & +19^\circ 50' 25.9'' \\ \hline \text{Rigel } (\beta\text{-Ori}) & 5\text{h } 14\text{min } 32.3\text{s} & -08^\circ 12' 05.9'' \\ \hline \text{Polaris } (\alpha\text{-UMi}) & 2\text{h } 31\text{min } 50.2\text{s} & +89^\circ 15' 51.3'' \\ \hline \text{Regulus } (\alpha\text{-Leo}) & 10\text{h } 08\text{min } 22\text{s} & +11^\circ 58' 02.8'' \\ \hline \text{Arcturus } (\alpha\text{-Boo}) & 14\text{h } 15\text{min } 38.6\text{s} & +19^\circ 10' 22.1'' \\ \hline \text{Pollux } (\beta\text{-Gem}) & 7\text{h } 45\text{min } 18.2\text{s} & +28^\circ 01' 31.7'' \\ \hline \text{Achernar } (\alpha\text{-Eri}) & 1\text{h } 37\text{min } 43\text{s} & -57^\circ 14' 13.3'' \\ \hline \end{array}$$

1) A planet is made up of three concentric spherical layers, the first being its spherical core. The volume of the core is equal to that of the intermediate layer surrounding it. The radius of the core is one quarter of the diameter of the planet and it has a density 9 times greater than that of water. The intermediate layer has a thickness of 519.84 km and a density 5 times greater than that of water. The outer layer has a density 3 times greater than that of water. Calculate the acceleration of gravity:

a) At the outer surface of the planet;

b) At the centre of the planet;

c) At the boundary between the second layer and the outer layer.

2) A star revolves around a black hole with its orbital plane parallel to the observer's plane of vision. It is observed that the rest line of 5985 Å varies from 5975 Å to 6000 Å, where the maximum redshift occurs in 3 days and the maximum blueshift occurs in 4 days, repeating cyclically. Calculate:

a) Its orbital period around the black hole;

b) The maximum and minimum velocities;

c) The eccentricity of its orbit.

3) Two binary star systems, one twenty light years away and the other sixty light years away, are superposed in the sky. By certain studies it is determined that the apparent magnitudes of the first two are 1 and 2, and of the last two 3 and 4. Calculate:

a) The absolute magnitude of each system;

b) The apparent magnitude of the four stars.
